---
title: Lemon Chickpea Soup
servings: 4
prep time: 20 minutes
cook time: 35 minutes
source: Rainbow Plant Life (Nisha Vora), https://rainbowplantlife.com/lemony-chickpea-soup/
tags: [soup, vegan, chickpea]
---

== Prep Ahead: For the Base ==

Rinse @long-grain white rice{1/3%cup} until water runs clear; drain.

Finely chop @carrots{4} and @yellow onion{1}.

Finely chop @garlic{5%cloves}.

Measure @dried oregano{2%tsp}, @ground coriander{1/2%tsp}, @ground turmeric{1/2%tsp}, and @red pepper flakes{1/4%tsp} into one bowl.

Drain and rinse @chickpeas{2%cans}, keeping one can separate for the puree.

== Prep Ahead: For the Puree ==

Zest and juice @lemon{1} (3-4 tbsp juice). Best done close to cook time; it loses brightness after a day.

Measure @plant milk{1/2%cup}, @nutritional yeast{1/4%cup}, and @white miso{1%tbsp}.

== Prep Ahead: For Serving ==

Chop @fresh dill{3/4%cup} and @baby spinach{3%oz}.

== Cooking Day ==

Heat @olive oil{1/4%cup} in a #large pot{} over medium-high heat. Add the @carrots{} and @yellow onion{}, season with @salt{} and @black pepper{}, and cook for ~{10%minutes} until softened.

Add the @garlic{} and cook ~{2%minutes}. Add @tomato paste{2%tbsp} and the spice blend; stir ~{2%minutes} until darker red, deglazing with a splash of @vegetable broth{} if it sticks.

Add the rinsed @long-grain white rice{} and toss ~{1%minute}. Add one can of @chickpeas{}, @salt{}, and @black pepper{}. Pour in @vegetable broth{4%cups} and half the @fresh dill{}. Bring to a boil.

Reduce to a simmer, cover, and cook ~{15%minutes} until the rice is soft.

Meanwhile, blend the second can of chickpeas with the @plant milk{}, @lemon{} zest, 2 tbsp lemon juice, @nutritional yeast{}, and @white miso{} until smooth.

Stir the puree and chopped @baby spinach{} into the soup. Simmer ~{2%minutes} until thickened and the greens are wilted.

Off heat, stir in the remaining @fresh dill{} and 1 tbsp @lemon{} juice. Season to taste and serve with a drizzle of @olive oil{}.

> Keeps 5-6 days refrigerated, freezes 3-4 months. Brighten leftovers with a squeeze of lemon and a drizzle of olive oil when reheating.
